Confirmation 1 — flag combinations across the ten files: no public case sets both no_empty_before and no_empty_after simultaneously, and no public case combines cycle with either no_empty flag. The combo rule is already on the table from the cycle round: cycle takes precedence over both no_empty_before and no_empty_after — at a boundary, wrap wins regardless. Each no_empty flag gates only its own direction's creation branch, so if you need behavior for both-set-at-once, derive it from those two disclosed facts; no public case pins it down further.

Confirmation 2 — monitor_ids ordering: no. Every public case delivers monitor_ids already in ascending order. That is incidental, not contractual: input order is guaranteed not to matter — the core sorts them internally and steps through the sorted order. If your implementation indexes into the list as-given, features 1–10 will pass and hidden cases will break you. Sort defensively.

That closes the ten-file set. All cards for navigation semantics, boundaries, cycle priority, the stdin schema, and the output contract are now on the table; the error <category> tokens and exit codes remain unspecified — treat per the fallback guidance already given.
